### AI_EXPORT — the one endpoint this feature exposes, plus its refusals.
###
### REST Client format; runs from the IDE with no external tool. It versions with the code, so it
### never drifts from the route it probes (QUALITY-01). The round-trip test is the guarantee that runs
### in CI; this is the manual probe for when something still surprises you.
###
### Run the requests in order. The session and the cross-site token are both cookies, so the client
### carries them forward on its own once request 1 has been refused — that refusal is what mints the
### token, read back out of the jar with {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}.
###
### **The response is a zip archive, not JSON.** Most REST clients will offer to save it. Open it and
### you get one JSONL file per record type: header, tasks, instances, steps, conversions. There is no
### templates.jsonl and that is deliberate — TASKLIB is not built, and an absent record type says the
### feature does not exist where an empty file would say the workspace has no templates.
###
### Two things to know before the answers look wrong. The export is **owner only and non-delegable**,
### so a manager gets 403 NOT_PERMITTED however senior they are; the archive is the whole workspace's
### history and cannot be scoped once it has left the building. And a workspace with no tasks answers
### **409 NO_HISTORY** rather than producing empty files, because five empty files look like a complete
### export of a business that has done nothing.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase

### 1. Who am I? 401 before signing in — and the cookie it sets is the point.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/session

### 2. Sign in as the owner. Expect 200 and a SESSION cookie.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "{{owner}}", "password": "{{password}}"}

### 3. Export everything. Expect 200 and a zip. An empty body means the whole history.
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{}

### 4. Export a window. A task created **before** `from` but closed inside it is included — excluding
### it would silently drop exactly the long-running work an analysis most wants (UC_01 extension 1a).
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"from": "2026-05-01T00:00:00Z", "to": "2026-07-01T00:00:00Z"}

### 5. Take it twice and compare any `assignee_ref` between the two files. They **differ**, for the
### same person, because the pseudonym is salted per export. That is what makes following one
### individual across two files impossible rather than merely disallowed (AI_EXPORT_03 section 3).
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{}

### --- Failure probes. Each one exists so a live 403 versus 409 versus 500 is one keystroke away. ---

### 6. As somebody who is not the owner. Sign in as an employee first, then send this.
### Expect 403 NOT_PERMITTED. A 500 here means this feature's exception handler has lost its
### AuthorizationDeniedException arm — which is exactly how this endpoint first behaved, and the
### round trip is what found it.
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{}

### 7. Against a workspace with no tasks. Expect 409 NO_HISTORY and no archive.
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{}

### 8. No session at all. Expect 401 from the security chain, with no body.
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### --- AI-EXPORT-SCOPED-01: one template's history, so a finding can be checked by hand ---

### 9. Scope the export to one process template. Expect 200 and a smaller archive carrying the same
### record shapes — one code path produces both, which is why a person checking a claim against a
### scoped file is reading the dataset the claim came from.
###
### Against the demo seed this answers 7 instances, 33 steps and 33 tasks where the full export
### answers 12, 61 and 92. Group steps.jsonl by origin=attached and you get "5 of 7", which is the
### sentence the insight card shows.
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export/scoped
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"subjectType": "process_template", "subjectId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-TEMPLATE-id"}

### 10. A subject nobody authored. Expect 404 NOT_FOUND — byte for byte what an unpermitted caller
### gets, because a different answer for "does not exist" and "not yours" tells an unpermitted caller
### which templates exist.
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export/scoped
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"subjectType": "process_template", "subjectId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"}

### 11. A task template. Valid in the specification, and unanswerable until TASKLIB is built — so it
### answers 404 like any other subject this product cannot find, rather than naming the gap.
POST {{host}}/api/ai-export/scoped
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"subjectType": "task_template", "subjectId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"}

### 12. Take a scoped export and a full one and compare any `assignee_ref` between them. They differ,
### because the pseudonym is salted per export — so a scoped file and a full one **cannot be joined
### to re-identify anybody**. That property is deliberate rather than incidental (UC_02 security).
